WEBVTT

NOTE Scene 03. Speaker labels and dialogue derive from the original script.
NOTE Time positions aligned using faster-whisper word timestamps; human review still required.

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.380
<v Jack Marlowe>By the time I returned, Betty had turned my desk into a small newspaper archive.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:09.360
<v Jack Marlowe>Vivian was waiting beside it, looking considerably more expensive than the furniture.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:14.254
<v Betty>While you were out playin' detective, some of us did actual woik.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:17.160
<v Jack Marlowe>I was under the impression that's what I was doing.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:18.620
<v Betty>That's adorable.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:21.937
<v Jack Marlowe>What have you got on Sterling?

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:26.720
<v Betty>Three insurance companies, two bankrupt businesses, four lawsuits, and a picture

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.700
<v Betty>of him openin' a children's hospital.

00:00:28.700 --> 00:00:30.860
<v Jack Marlowe>A philanthropist?

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:33.620
<v Betty>Hospital went bankrupt six months later.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.560
<v Vivian Cross>Ernest used to audit Sterling's accounts. He was always careful with money.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:43.480
<v Betty>I checked the key. It's stamped with the name of the Grand Central parcel storage

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.864
<v Betty>office. Locker forty-seven.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:47.700
<v Jack Marlowe>Get your coats, ladies.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:52.720
<v Jack Marlowe>Twenty minutes later, the three of us were standing in front of a steel locker,

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:57.660
<v Jack Marlowe>which was about the only thing in Grand Central that hadn't tried to sell me a

00:00:57.660 --> 00:00:58.200
<v Jack Marlowe>newspaper.

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.380
<v Betty>Jack! There's a whole book in here. And an envelope.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:08.520
<v Jack Marlowe>A ledger. Look at these figures. Sterling was collecting insurance premiums, transferring

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:12.199
<v Jack Marlowe>the money into his own accounts, and declaring the companies insolvent.

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:15.080
<v Vivian Cross>That's Ernest's handwriting on the envelope.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:17.860
<v Jack Marlowe>It's addressed to you. Go ahead.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.460
<v Vivian Cross>My dearest Vivian. If you're reading this, I've gone into hiding.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.920
<v Vivian Cross>Sterling discovered that I copied his accounts.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:31.240
<v Vivian Cross>I left my coat by the river to make him believe I was gone.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:36.380
<v Vivian Cross>The ledger is my insurance. Contact my attorney when it's safe.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:39.580
<v Vivian Cross>Forgive me. All my love, Ernest.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:41.120
<v Betty>He's alive!

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:44.380
<v Vivian Cross>That miserable, inconsiderate little man!

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:47.440
<v Betty>Gee, lady. Most wives would be happy.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.560
<v Vivian Cross>He could have left a note!

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:51.560
<v Jack Marlowe>Technically, he did.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:56.700
<v Vivian Cross>I'm going to kiss him. And then I'm going to kill him.

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:00.160
<v Betty>That'll save us a lotta paperwork next time.

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.460
<v Jack Marlowe>Betty, take the ledger to the attorney named on the letter.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:07.300
<v Jack Marlowe>Ask for certified copies, and have him contact the district attorney.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:08.980
<v Jack Marlowe>Keep the original safe.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.383
<v Betty>You got it, boss. What about Sterling?

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:16.840
<v Jack Marlowe>The ledger proves the money was stolen. A confession will make it harder for him

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:17.740
<v Jack Marlowe>to blame Ernest.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:21.060
<v Vivian Cross>Sterling would do almost anything to get that hat back.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.160
<v Vivian Cross>I could offer to sell it to him.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:28.600
<v Betty>Or Jack could just walk into his office and irritate him until he spills the beans.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:30.320
<v Betty>That's his specialty.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:37.200
<v Jack Marlowe>We had the evidence, Ernest had a way home, and Sterling didn't know

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:43.260
<v Jack Marlowe>his empire was about to collapse. I could confront him in his office, or let

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:47.840
<v Jack Marlowe>Vivian arrange a little business transaction he wouldn't soon forget.

